module lib/minpack/enorm  documents every variable
  The Euclidean norm of a vector, and the two machine constants MINPACK
  reads from dpmpar.

  EPSMCH and DWARF are dpmpar(1) and dpmpar(2) exactly as netlib's dpmpar.f
  writes them for IEEE machines, to 11 digits. They are close to, but not
  equal to, 2^-52 and the smallest normal double, and the port uses the
  Fortran's values so that its tolerance tests take the same branches.

enorm  @dcf191ff
  what  $\sqrt{x_0^2 + \cdots + x_{n-1}^2}$ for the first n entries of x,
        without overflow or underflow for any representable entries.
  how   Line by line: each entry goes into one of three sums by size.
        Entries between RDWARF and $r_{\mathrm{giant}} / n$ are squared and added
        directly (s2): their squares, and n of them added, stay in range.
        Larger entries go to s1 and smaller nonzero ones to s3, each summed
        as squares relative to the largest entry of its class seen so far
        (x1max, x3max); when a new largest arrives, the old sum is rescaled
        by $(\text{old max} / \text{new max})^2$ before adding $1$. At the end, if any
        large entry was seen, the result is $x_{\max,1} \sqrt{s_1 + s_2 / x_{\max,1}^2}$
        (the small ones cannot matter); otherwise the intermediate sum,
        with the small ones folded in on whichever scale is larger, or the
        small ones alone.
  why   $\sum x_i^2$ computed directly overflows for entries near $10^{200}$ and
        flushes entries near $10^{-200}$ to zero; enorm gets both right to rounding
        [test "enorm of 1e200 and 1e-200 entries does not overflow or underflow"],
        and for ordinary vectors it agrees with the plain formula
        [test "for ordinary numbers enorm is the plain square root of the sum of squares"].
        The Fortran's order of operations is kept, since lmder's iterates
        are compared with the Fortran's bit for bit.
  x       $x$  the vector
  xabs    $|x_i|$  the size of the current entry
  s1      $s_1$  the sum of squares of the large entries, relative to the largest
  s2      $s_2$  the sum of squares of the ordinary entries
  s3      $s_3$  the sum of squares of the tiny entries, relative to the largest of them
  x1max   $x_{\max,1}$  the largest large entry so far
  x3max   $x_{\max,3}$  the largest tiny entry so far
  RDWARF  $r_{\mathrm{dwarf}}$  below this, a square could underflow
  RGIANT  $r_{\mathrm{giant}}$  above this over n, a sum of n squares could overflow
  agiant  $a_{\mathrm{giant}}$  $r_{\mathrm{giant}} / n$, the largest entry counted as ordinary for this n
  n       $n$  how many entries of x to use
  i       the entry being added in
  q       a ratio at most 1, squared to put a sum on one scale: the old largest
          over the new largest, or the entry over the largest of its class

  $$ \lVert x \rVert = \sqrt{\textstyle\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} x_i^2} $$  [test "for ordinary numbers enorm is the plain square root of the sum of squares"]
  $$ \lVert x \rVert = x_{\max,1} \sqrt{s_1 + \frac{s_2}{x_{\max,1}^2}} $$  [test "enorm of 1e200 and 1e-200 entries does not overflow or underflow"]
  = agiant  the upper limit of an ordinary entry for this n: n squares below it add up to at most $r_{\mathrm{giant}}^2 \approx 1.7 \times 10^{38}$, which fits even in single precision, the smallest range MINPACK was written for
  = s2#2   an entry of ordinary size: its square is added as it is
  = s3#2   a tiny entry larger than every tiny one before it: the old sum is rescaled to the new largest, and this entry adds 1
  = s1#2   a large entry larger than every large one before it: the same rescaling, relative to the largest large entry
